    "description": "Iran’s weightlifting history includes internationally prominent champions such as Hossein Rezazadeh, whose Olympic victories helped make the super-heavyweight platform a stage for national sporting recognition. Women’s participation has a much more recent institutional history, adding a distinct story of opportunity, achievement and athlete mobility.",
    "tags": ["Weightlifting", "Olympic sport", "Hossein Rezazadeh", "Women in sport", "Strength sports"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:06:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:06:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Weightlifting in Iran: Champions, Competition and Women’s Participation",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s weightlifting tradition, Hossein Rezazadeh’s Olympic legacy, competition structure and the more recent development of women’s lifting."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "sport-and-platform",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A sport decided attempt by attempt",
        "content": "For an international reader, Iranian weightlifting is best approached through both the competition platform and the careers that have made it culturally visible. The International Weightlifting Federation, or IWF, organizes competition around the snatch and the clean and jerk. Its competition guide gives athletes three attempts in each lift, with the barbell progressing upward as competitors and coaches declare their chosen weights. The sequence makes tactics visible: an athlete’s prospects depend on selecting and completing attempts, rather than simply demonstrating strength in training. [s1]\n\nThe IWF guide also distinguishes awards for the individual lifts from the total. That distinction matters when reading championship reports: an individual-lift medal and a total medal describe different achievements. Results should therefore be read with the event, bodyweight category and award type attached. A headline about a medal, without those details, gives an incomplete picture of the competition. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rezazadeh-legacy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hossein Rezazadeh and the super-heavyweight spotlight",
        "content": "Hossein Rezazadeh offers a particularly clear entry into Iran’s international weightlifting history. The IWF’s retrospective traces his emergence through Asian and world competition before his Olympic victory at Sydney in 2000. He won again at Athens in 2004 and accumulated four world championship victories between 2002 and 2006. These results explain his prominence more precisely than an unsourced claim that Iran has always dominated the sport. [s2]\n\nThe super-heavyweight division carries a distinctive public symbolism: its leading lifters are associated with exceptional absolute strength. The IWF explicitly places Rezazadeh within that tradition of sporting celebrity. His career also illustrates the movement of successful athletes into sporting administration. After retiring before the Beijing Olympics in 2008 on medical advice, he became involved with the Iranian federation in advisory, management and coaching roles, according to the retrospective. [s2]\n\nHis achievements remain a historical reference point. They should not be used as a substitute for examining the results of later generations or as evidence about the present availability of coaching and facilities. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "womens-participation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A newer history for women",
        "content": "Women’s weightlifting has a substantially different timeline. An IWF report published in 2024 states that women were not permitted to practise the sport in Iran until 2018. The same report follows Yekta Jamali, an emerging Iranian lifter who left for Germany in 2022 and subsequently competed for the IWF Weightlifting Refugee Team. It attributes the explanation that she left to continue practising safely to the International Olympic Committee. That attribution should be preserved rather than presented as an independently established account of every circumstance surrounding her departure. [s3]\n\nJamali’s experience shows why an athlete’s Iranian background, training location and competition representation must be distinguished. It also makes women’s weightlifting more than an appendix to the history of male champions. A useful account asks who can enter the sport, how careers develop and which organization an athlete represents at a particular event. The inspected evidence supports these individual and institutional developments; it does not establish a national participation rate or demonstrate equal access to training. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
