    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Taekwondo has given Iran prominent Olympic champions and a significant stage for women’s sporting achievement. Hadi Saei’s successive Olympic medals and the Iranian team’s Paris 2024 results provide distinct reference points for understanding that history.",
    "tags": ["Taekwondo", "Olympic sport", "Hadi Saei", "Women in sport", "Paris 2024"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:06:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:06:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Taekwondo in Iran: Hadi Saei and a New Olympic Generation",
      "description": "Explore Iranian taekwondo through Hadi Saei’s Olympic career, the Paris 2024 medallists and the distinction between sporting identity and national representation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "olympic-reference-points",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading Iran’s taekwondo story through athletes",
        "content": "Iran’s taekwondo history becomes more concrete when individual careers replace broad claims about national sporting character. World Taekwondo, the international governing body whose results database records Olympic competition, documents Hadi Saei’s bronze medal at Sydney in 2000 and gold medals at Athens in 2004 and Beijing in 2008. His results span successive editions of the Games, giving readers a clear historical foundation for understanding Iran’s standing in the sport. [s1]\n\nThe categories matter as well as the medals. Saei’s Sydney and Athens results appear in the men’s under-68-kilogram category, while his Beijing title appears in the under-80-kilogram category. His career therefore cannot be reduced to repeated victories in an unchanged division. The official profile supplies the event-level detail needed to keep those achievements distinct. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "paris-generation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Paris 2024 generation",
        "content": "Paris provided a different picture: success spread across women’s and men’s events. World Taekwondo’s Iran results page records gold for Arian Salimi in men’s over-80-kilogram competition, silver for Mehran Barkhordari in men’s under-80-kilogram competition, silver for Nahid Kiyanichandeh in women’s under-57-kilogram competition and bronze for Mobina Nematzadeh in women’s under-49-kilogram competition. The country summary records one gold, two silvers and one bronze. [s2]\n\nThese results make the women’s achievements integral to the account. They also show why a country’s sporting performance should be examined across events: the Paris medals were earned by different competitors in different categories, rather than by a single exceptional athlete. Yet the results establish outcomes at an elite tournament. They do not, by themselves, establish how many people practise taekwondo in Iran, how opportunities vary geographically or how accessible coaching is outside the national team. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "identity-and-representation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Iranian identity and competition representation",
        "content": "The women’s under-57-kilogram results also contain an important distinction for readers following Iranian athletes abroad. The French Olympic team’s Paris results service lists Kiyanichandeh as the Iranian silver medallist and Kimia Alizadeh as a bronze medallist representing Bulgaria. Their presence in the same event is a useful reminder that an athlete’s background and the delegation appearing beside her name are separate pieces of information. Alizadeh’s Paris medal belongs to Bulgaria’s competition record. [s3]\n\nContemporary reporting by Le Monde situates their Paris achievements within a longer personal and political story. It recounts Alizadeh’s earlier Olympic medal for Iran, her departure after criticizing the authorities, and her later representation of the Refugee Olympic Team and Bulgaria. That account describes a particular athlete’s experience; it should not be generalized into a single explanation for every sporting migration. [s4]\n\nTogether, these careers offer an introduction with two scales: a durable record of Olympic achievement and individual lives that cross national-team boundaries. Keeping both in view gives a fuller understanding than a medal table alone. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
