  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-13-2",
    "page_path": "/explore/sport/traditional-sports/",
    "name": "Traditional Sports",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/sport/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s traditional sports encompass gymnasium exercise, wrestling, mounted games and locally transmitted forms of play. Their significance lies in the connections between physical skill, poetry, music, community occasions and regional identity, while their histories show continuing change rather than an unbroken, uniform national tradition.",
    "tags": ["Traditional sports", "Zurkhaneh", "Chogan", "Wrestling", "Intangible heritage", "Regional culture"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:06:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:06:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Traditional Sports in Iran: Zurkhaneh, Chogan and Regional Games",
      "description": "Discover Iran’s traditional physical cultures, from zurkhaneh exercise and chogan to regional wrestling, community performance and locally transmitted games."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "plural-traditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A family of practices",
        "content": "Traditional sport in Iran is a useful umbrella term, but it covers practices with different settings and purposes. A gymnasium session accompanied by poetry, a mounted ball game and a wrestling display at a local celebration do not belong to one standardized competitive system. UNESCO’s descriptions of Pahlevani and Zoorkhanei rituals and Chogān show how athletic activity can be inseparable from performance, ethical teaching and transmission between generations. [s1][s2]\n\nThe broader vocabulary of games is wider still. Encyclopaedia Iranica’s historical survey discusses children’s activities, ceremonial play, theatrical games and games of intelligence. This page concentrates on physical practices while recognizing that the boundary between sport, recreation and public entertainment is porous. Historical descriptions help explain those connections; they do not establish that every documented activity remains common today. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "gymnasium-and-wrestling",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Zurkhaneh exercise and the ideal of the pahlavan",
        "content": "Zurkhaneh means “house of strength.” The term names a traditional gymnasium as well as evoking the physical culture associated with it. UNESCO describes coordinated exercise accompanied by a morshed, a master who drums and recites poetry. Its account links physical discipline with ethical instruction and community assistance, while explicitly identifying Islamic, mystical and older Persian elements in the tradition. These are aspects of the documented practice, rather than decorative symbols added to it. UNESCO inscribed the rituals on its Representative List in 2010. [s1]\n\nThe gymnasium and wrestling should nevertheless be distinguished. Historian Houchang Chehabi’s Iranica entry explains that the zurkhaneh was closely associated with wrestling until the middle of the twentieth century, but that pahlavani wrestling subsequently became more separate from gymnasium exercise. He also cautions against treating the institution as timeless: architecture, routines and social roles have evolved. [s4]\n\nThis distinction organizes the branch’s separate pages. Zurkhaneh concerns the training environment, equipment, rhythm and collective practice; Pahlavani Wrestling concerns the wrestling tradition. They overlap historically, but neither is an interchangeable name for all Iranian wrestling. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mounted-game",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Chogan: riding, play and narration",
        "content": "Chogan is a mounted team game in which riders use wooden sticks to send a ball through the opposing side’s goalposts. UNESCO’s account treats the game together with its accompanying music and storytelling. Players, musicians and narrators are all identified as bearers of the heritage, making a description limited to the action on the field incomplete. The element was inscribed in 2017. [s2]\n\nTransmission is equally important. UNESCO describes learning within families and workshops, alongside associations that provide courses and support local masters. Its account therefore presents both informal continuity and organized safeguarding. The association model does not erase local diversity, and heritage recognition should not be read as a count of active players or proof that every community has equal access to horses and training. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-occasions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional sports and community occasions",
        "content": "Regional context changes what a sporting event means. In Gilan, the Caspian province, Iranica’s folklore account describes Gilani wrestling and tightrope performance within a broader world of public entertainment. Village commons and domestic courtyards could become performance spaces for celebrations and gatherings. Tightrope acts combined an athlete’s balancing skills with music and comic assistance, illustrating how physical accomplishment could be presented as a collective performance. [s5]\n\nThe same account describes egg contests associated with Nowruz, the spring new-year celebration. These belong to a different scale of play from mounted competition or wrestling, yet their preparation, agreed rules and competitive outcome make them relevant to the wider study of games. Local categories deserve attention on their own terms. [s5]\n\nThe pages on Indigenous Games, Regional Sports and Traditional Rural Competitions provide complementary approaches: inherited forms of play, geographically distinctive practices and the occasions that bring competitors and spectators together. These are editorial organizing lenses, not claims that every practice fits only one category. [s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-heritage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding continuity without freezing the past",
        "content": "The most useful questions are practical: who teaches a skill, where is it performed, what rules do participants recognize and what makes an occasion meaningful? Iranica’s games survey highlights the difficulty of documenting local variants systematically, while its zurkhaneh history demonstrates institutional change. Together they caution against presenting a single national rulebook or assuming that a historical description is a current participation survey. [s3][s4]\n\nTraditional sports are consequently best understood as practices sustained and reshaped by people. Recognition, scholarship and documentation make them easier to discover, but each answers a different question from whether a particular venue is operating or an event is scheduled today. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
